const aug13ResearchPost = (slug: string, title: string, excerpt: string, focus: string, evidence: string, decisions: string, published: string, limits = 'The available sources do not establish a universal operating threshold. Review the applicable business, jurisdiction, channel, and data context with the responsible owner.'): ResearchPost => ({
  slug, title, excerpt, published, sourceDate: published, sources: aug13ResearchSources,
  keyStats: ['8 authoritative sources reviewed', '4 decision variables to document', '1 named owner for every exception'],
  sections: [
    { heading: 'Question and method', body: `This brief examines ${focus}. It uses standards and public guidance as control evidence rather than treating any source as a performance claim about a particular provider. The unit of analysis is a customer-contact decision: the request presented, the information available at that moment, the action permitted, and the owner accountable for an exception. ISO 18295 provides a contact-centre lens for customer experience, people, processes, and results. NIST CSF 2.0, the Privacy Framework, Zero Trust Architecture, and Digital Identity Guidelines add governance, data-minimization, access, and assurance considerations. PCI SSC material is relevant when payment data enters the interaction. FTC and FCC material matters when the contact is outbound or consent-sensitive. The comparison is deliberately bounded: these sources describe safeguards and obligations, not a universal staffing model or guaranteed customer outcome. The useful question is therefore not whether a queue is outsourced, but whether its scope, evidence, decision rights, and escalation owner remain visible at the point of service.`, },
    { heading: 'Findings', body: `${evidence} A practical finding follows from that evidence: the customer-facing worker should receive only the information needed for the defined request, while the decision owner should receive enough context to resolve exceptions without replaying the entire interaction. That distinction matters because speed and safety are not opposites; they are managed through different boundaries. A short interaction can still create a large downstream risk if a promise, identity decision, or consent record is unclear. Conversely, a longer interaction may be justified when the customer impact or data sensitivity is high. Reviewers should examine a defined cohort, such as one queue during one week, and separate ordinary contacts from exceptions. Useful measures include contacts by reason, unresolved cases, transfers, repeat contacts, missed promises, and records lacking an owner. Counts should be interpreted with volume, channel, time zone, and case severity, not presented as a standalone quality score.`, },
    { heading: 'Interpretation for service leaders', body: `${decisions} The strongest design makes the safe path the easiest path: one authoritative record, a small number of permitted actions, an observable handoff, and a known fallback when a fact is missing. Access should be individual and role-based, not inherited from a shared account or a broad administrative role. The service owner should define what can be answered, changed, scheduled, or merely recorded. A manager should own policy interpretation, sensitive-data questions, and exceptions that exceed the ordinary scope. Before expanding a queue, compare customer impact with review capacity. If unresolved exceptions, repeated transfers, or unowned callbacks rise while ordinary contact counts look stable, the decision is not simply to add volume. It may be to narrow scope, clarify the record, or add owner coverage.`, },
    { heading: 'Limitations and conclusion', body: `${limits} The sources do not establish a single threshold for acceptable response time, a universal permission matrix, or a guaranteed result from outsourcing. Legal requirements can vary by jurisdiction, product, customer, channel, and data category; the client’s counsel and compliance owner must resolve those questions. The evidence does support a conservative conclusion: a support service is more dependable when its customer promise, data boundary, authority limit, and escalation owner are explicit and reviewable. Leaders should record the decision variables, inspect a representative period, and revise the design when the evidence shows scope drift. The conclusion is bounded but useful: safe customer contact comes from visible ownership and proportionate controls, not from a label applied to the delivery model.`, },
    { heading: 'Manager questions', body: 'Which customer reasons are actually in scope? What evidence is sufficient for the permitted action? Which fields must never be copied into notes or messages? What is the first safe response when the record is incomplete? Who owns an exception, and when must a backup be notified? Which cohort and period will be reviewed before the service is expanded? These questions turn the research into decisions without pretending that a standard supplies the client-specific answer.' },
  ],
});

const supersededAug13ResearchPosts: readonly ResearchPost[] = [
  aug13ResearchPost('call-center-customer-consent-capture-a-research-brief', 'Call Center Customer Consent Capture: A Research Brief', 'Consent records are useful only when purpose, channel, timing, and ownership remain connected to the customer choice.', 'capturing and maintaining customer consent during support and permitted outbound contact', 'Privacy guidance favors purpose limitation and traceable choices, while FTC and FCC guidance show that outbound contact rules depend on channel, jurisdiction, consent status, and opt-out handling. A checkbox or verbal “yes” is not a complete record unless the business can identify what the customer agreed to, when, through which channel, and for which purpose.', 'Separate service contact from promotional contact, record the source and effective time in the approved system, and make opt-out or correction requests visible to the owner responsible for honoring them. Do not infer marketing permission from an inbound support request. When a customer asks what a choice means, route interpretation to the policy owner instead of improvising.', '2026-08-13', 'This is not a legal opinion and does not resolve jurisdiction-specific consent rules. The research also cannot determine whether a particular record is valid without the business context, notice language, and system history.'),
  aug13ResearchPost('call-center-customer-data-minimization-a-research-brief', 'Call Center Customer Data Minimization: A Research Brief', 'Data minimization in customer contact is a field-level design choice: collect what the request requires and no more.', 'limiting customer information exposed, repeated, or retained during ordinary call-centre service', 'The NIST Privacy Framework frames data processing around purpose and risk, while Zero Trust and PCI DSS materials support limiting access and protecting sensitive information. The relevant unit is the interaction field, not the customer record as a whole. A worker may need an order status but not a full payment number, or a callback preference but not unrelated account history.', 'Create a field map for each contact reason: required to identify the case, required to complete the permitted action, restricted to an authorized owner, and unnecessary. Mask or exclude secrets from notes and recordings. Review exports and attachments as well as the main screen, because copied data can outlive the original interaction and broaden exposure.', '2026-08-13', 'Minimization cannot be judged from word count alone. Some regulated or safety-related cases require more context, and retention, deletion, and access duties depend on the applicable business and jurisdiction.'),
  aug13ResearchPost('call-center-customer-verification-failure-research-brief', 'Call Center Customer Verification Failure: A Research Brief', 'A failed verification is a risk signal that should narrow disclosure and clarify the next safe owner.', 'what customer-contact teams should do when identity evidence is incomplete, inconsistent, or unavailable', 'NIST Digital Identity Guidelines distinguish assurance from convenience, and Zero Trust Architecture treats authorization as contextual rather than inherited. That supports separating low-risk general information from account-specific changes. A mismatch in name, address, callback number, or authentication response should not be “solved” by guessing which record looks closest.', 'Define the disclosure limit after a failed check, the approved alternate evidence, retry limits, and the owner for unresolved cases. Tell the customer what can happen next without revealing the expected answer or unnecessary account details. Record the outcome and reason category, not secrets. Review repeated failures by cohort and period to distinguish fraud signals, stale records, accessibility barriers, and confusing verification questions.', '2026-08-13', 'The sources do not prescribe a single factor or retry count for every service. Accessibility, fraud risk, account type, and local requirements may require different approved paths.'),
  aug13ResearchPost('call-center-callback-promise-reliability-a-research-brief', 'Call Center Callback Promise Reliability: A Research Brief', 'A callback promise is a customer-impact commitment whose reliability depends on time zone, owner, channel, and fallback evidence.', 'the reliability of scheduled callbacks and the conditions that make a callback promise actionable', 'ISO 18295 emphasizes contact processes and results, while identity and privacy guidance limit what can be disclosed before the recipient is verified. A callback record therefore needs more than a phone number. It should include the customer’s local time zone, permitted channel, promised window, request, verification state, and responsible owner. A count of attempted calls cannot distinguish a safe attempt from an unusable promise.', 'Measure promised callbacks in a defined period by completed within window, missed, unreachable, wrong-channel, and unresolved. Require a next action when the window passes. Keep voicemail neutral and do not place sensitive account facts in an unverified message. Escalate high-impact missed promises according to customer impact rather than handle-time pressure.', 'Callback success rates are not comparable without common definitions for window, attempt, customer availability, and case severity. A short observation period may also miss seasonal demand.'),
  aug13ResearchPost('call-center-customer-complaint-routing-a-research-brief', 'Call Center Customer Complaint Routing: A Research Brief', 'Complaint routing should preserve customer impact and ownership instead of turning a serious concern into an untracked transfer.', 'how complaint intake and routing affect customer outcomes, escalation visibility, and decision ownership', 'Contact-centre standards emphasize defined processes and outcomes; NIST governance and incident guidance emphasize roles, communication, and evidence. The complaint itself is not merely a sentiment label. It may contain a missed promise, safety concern, billing dispute, accessibility barrier, or request for a policy decision. Routing that removes the original context makes the next owner repeat questions and can conceal unresolved harm.', 'Record the customer’s stated issue, impact, requested remedy, time sensitivity, prior action, and next owner. Distinguish acknowledgment from resolution. Use severity and promised update time to order review, while keeping refunds, credits, legal interpretation, and policy exceptions with the authorized owner. Sample closed complaints for whether the outcome answered the stated issue, not only whether a status code was entered.', 'The research does not define a universal severity taxonomy or promise that every complaint can be resolved by the first owner. Remedies depend on the business policy and facts of the case.'),
  aug13ResearchPost('call-center-appointment-reschedule-controls-a-research-brief', 'Call Center Appointment Reschedule Controls: A Research Brief', 'Rescheduling is a service decision involving identity, availability, customer promises, and a durable change to the record.', 'the control questions surrounding appointment changes made through customer contact', 'Privacy and identity guidance support verifying the requester before a sensitive change, while ISO 18295 supports clear process ownership and measurable outcomes. A reschedule may affect staff capacity, customer access, cancellation terms, and downstream reminders. Treating it as a simple calendar edit hides the difference between an authorized change and an accidental reassignment.', 'Define which appointment fields may be changed, the verification required, the time window, and the exception owner. Show the old and new appointment state in the record, preserve the customer’s time zone, and send only approved confirmation content. Review duplicate, late, and abandoned reschedules separately from ordinary completed contacts because they reveal different causes.', 'Scheduling rules, cancellation rights, and privacy requirements vary by service. The sources support control questions but cannot establish the correct local appointment policy.'),
  aug13ResearchPost('call-center-queue-backlog-aging-a-research-brief', 'Call Center Queue Backlog Aging: A Research Brief', 'Backlog age is a customer-impact signal only when age, severity, promise, dependency, and ownership are read together.', 'how aging work should be interpreted when a support queue contains both ordinary and escalated cases', 'ISO 18295 supports attention to customer-contact results, and NIST governance guidance supports named responsibility and risk-based decisions. A backlog count alone can reward closing easy records while older or blocked cases remain. Age should be measured from a defined event, such as receipt or promised update, and paired with customer impact, current owner, next action, and blocking dependency.', 'Segment the queue into age bands and case classes, then review the oldest high-impact work first. Separate waiting on customer, waiting on internal decision, and actively owned work. Set an acknowledgment threshold and a backup owner for overdue escalations. Compare age distribution with offered contacts and staffing intervals before concluding that volume alone caused the backlog.', 'There is no universal safe age threshold. A low-volume queue can contain a more serious risk than a high-volume queue, and definitions must be agreed before trend comparisons.'),
  aug13ResearchPost('call-center-supervisor-coverage-research-brief', 'Call Center Supervisor Coverage: A Research Brief', 'Supervisor coverage is the capacity to make timely customer-impact, access, quality, and exception decisions during the service period.', 'the relationship between supervisor availability and safe frontline customer-contact decisions', 'Contact-centre process guidance recognizes people and governance as part of service results. NIST frameworks likewise place accountability, incident response, and access decisions with named roles. The important measure is not a universal agent-to-supervisor ratio; it is whether the required review and escalation work can be completed within the customer promise. Coverage must include absences, handoffs, incidents, and policy questions.', 'Map supervisor duties by interval: live escalation, access decision, customer-impact exception, record review, and communication with the client owner. Name a backup and define a pause or narrowing rule when neither can respond within the required window. Review unresolved escalations and delayed approvals by shift, not only by individual worker.', 'A ratio cannot be transferred across channels, case severity, hours, or authority limits. Employment, labor, and contractual questions require the responsible business and legal owners.'),
  aug13ResearchPost('call-center-customer-record-correction-a-research-brief', 'Call Center Customer Record Correction: A Research Brief', 'Record correction protects service accuracy when the change is verified, attributable, reversible where possible, and limited to the approved field.', 'how customer-contact teams should handle requests to correct account or contact information', 'Privacy principles support accuracy and purpose limitation, while Zero Trust and identity guidance support verifying the requester before authorizing a change. A correction can affect future contact, access, delivery, billing, or safety. It therefore needs a field-specific authority rule rather than a broad “update customer data” permission.', 'Classify fields by impact, define the evidence needed, record old and new values only where the policy permits, and preserve the actor and time of change. Keep sensitive or disputed corrections with the named owner. Confirm the result through an approved channel and review reversals, repeated changes, and mismatches for signals that the ordinary path is not adequate.', 'The source material does not tell a business which fields may be corrected by a frontline role. Data rights, retention, and identity requirements differ by jurisdiction and account type.'),
  aug13ResearchPost('call-center-customer-contact-reason-taxonomy-a-research-brief', 'Call Center Customer Contact-Reason Taxonomy: A Research Brief', 'A contact-reason taxonomy is valuable when categories describe the customer’s actual need and lead to a defined owner or next action.', 'the design of contact-reason categories for routing, reporting, and customer-history accuracy', 'ISO 18295 connects contact processes to measurable results, while NIST governance favors accountable definitions and traceable decisions. A category set should not force a complex interaction into an inaccurate label merely because the report has limited columns. The unit should be the primary customer need, with a separate indicator for escalation, outcome, and unresolved dependency when those facts matter.', 'Define each reason with inclusion and exclusion examples, the next owner, required evidence, and the permitted “unknown” path. Test the taxonomy against a representative cohort across channels and shifts. Review uncategorized, multi-reason, and frequently changed records; they often indicate an ambiguous definition rather than individual failure. Change the taxonomy through an owned decision so trend data remains interpretable.', 'Categories are not outcomes, and a neat distribution is not proof of service quality. The correct granularity depends on the decisions the business must make and the privacy of the underlying data.'),
];

